# React Conventions

## Tech Stack

- React 19 + TypeScript (strict) + Vite
- CSS Modules (`Component.module.css`) for component styles
- `@lib/i18n.js` `t()` for user-facing copy (loads `locales/*.json`); `react-i18next` only where already wired
- Shared `@shared/theme.css` and `@shared/animations.css` at popup, cockpit, and prompt entry points
- Vitest (+ happy-dom) for tests
- Background RPC + IndexedDB for durable/extension state — not a SPA data layer
- For i18n stack details (including content-script strings), see `internationalization.mdc`

## Core Principles

- Functional components and hooks only; TypeScript props interfaces; avoid new `any`
- Prefer composition over prop drilling; use React context only for cross-cutting popup/app state
- Prefer local state (`useState` / `useReducer`); avoid storing derived values in state
- Prefer default exports for UI components; named exports for utilities, context providers, and multi-export modules (`SectionLabel` / `SectionHint` named exports are intentional)
- File naming: `PascalCase` components, `camelCase` hooks/utilities; colocate component + `.module.css` (shared feature CSS files are fine, e.g. cockpit pages importing `CockpitApp.module.css`)
- Put reusable UI in `src/components/` (icons live in `src/assets/`); keep feature screens under `popup/`, `cockpit/`, or `prompt/`
- Extract reusable logic into hooks under `src/shared/hooks` when adding new shared statefulness
- Prefer `const` and early returns; keep diffs focused and match existing patterns

## Extension UI Boundaries

- Talk to the background via shared messaging (`rpc` / `rpcNotify`, or typed `ExtensionRequest` envelopes like `AttentionXPanel` and cockpit pages); never import signing code, vault secrets, or Nostr private keys into React trees
- Do not mount React in the X content-script Shadow DOM panel — that UI stays vanilla TypeScript
- Prefer `t('key')` for new user-facing strings; do not mass-migrate existing hardcoded copy unless you are already editing that component
- Keep controls accessible: meaningful labels, keyboard-reachable actions, and clear loading/error states

## Performance

- Existing `useMemo` / `useCallback` usage is fine — do not remove or add more unless there is a clear need
- `useEffectEvent`, `startTransition`, and `useDeferredValue` are optional for new work only — do not retrofit them into existing effects
- Lazy-load heavy screens with `React.lazy` + `Suspense` only when it clearly helps load time

## Forms & Testing

- Prefer controlled inputs and small local form state; do not add form libraries without asking
- Test important logic with Vitest as `name.test.ts`; component `.test.tsx` files are optional and not yet common here
- Prefer testing behavior and pure helpers over implementation details

## Forbidden / Avoid

- Do not introduce major libraries (router, query client, CSS framework, form lib) without asking
- Do not add new global CSS or inline styles when a static CSS module class would work; inline styles are fine for dynamic/computed values (position, animation, runtime URLs)
- Do not mutate state directly or create deeply nested component trees
- Do not assume React Server Components — this is a Chrome extension client UI
- Do not refactor unrelated components to match these rules in the same change. Same-concern siblings (parallel screens, locales, tests for the behavior you changed) are in scope. Do not grow business rules or compensating data shapes in React when the bone or user-layer should change instead. Data and business code follows `data-business-clean-code.mdc`; React stays presentation.
- Do not split large existing screens (`AttentionXPanel`, cockpit pages, `PermissionsSection`, `KeyActionModal`, etc.) unless the task is an explicit refactor
- Do not clean up existing `any` or convert existing inline styles unless that file’s change requires it
- Do not convert `ExtensionRequest` / `axRequest` call sites to generic `rpc` (or the reverse) unless that is the task

## When making changes

1. Understand existing patterns in the codebase
2. Match the current style and architecture
3. Update or add tests when changing behavior
4. Keep diffs focused on the **concern**; include family siblings; do not expand into unrelated refactors
